<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wars of the Roses</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1455">1455</a>–<a href="/years/1487">1487</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wars of the Roses were a series of civil wars for the English throne between the houses of Lancaster and York, branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenet dynasty</a>, from 1455 to 1487. Weak rule under Henry VI led to rival claims, and the crown changed hands several times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> at Bosworth in 1485 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">Tudor dynasty</a>, and his win at Stoke in 1487 ended the wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses.jpg" alt="Wars of the Roses" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ghent master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beheading_duke_somerset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The wars were known at the time and for long afterwards as the Civil Wars. The name Wars of the Roses refers to the white rose of York and the red rose of Lancaster, which was the Lancastrian emblem by 1485. Historians disagree over the main causes. They point to the economic and social troubles that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and to the powerful duchies Edward III created for his sons, which fed a bastard feudalism in which great lords kept private forces. The succession after Edward III's death in 1377 is held by the historian Ian Mortimer to be the root cause.</p><p>Henry IV took the throne in 1399 from Richard II, basing his right on descent in the male line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>, though Edmund Mortimer, descended through Lionel, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarence</a>, could be argued to be the heir presumptive. The mental instability of Henry VI revived the interest of his cousin Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, 3rd Duke of York, in a claim of his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting began on 22 May 1455 at the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>, where York captured the king and several of his rivals were killed, among them Edmund Beaufort, 2nd Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>. After Richard Neville, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>, took Henry again at Northampton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> was killed at Wakefield, and his 18-year-old son Edward inherited the claim. Edward defeated the Lancastrians at Towton and was crowned on 28 June 1461.</p><p>Edward IV's secret marriage to Elizabeth Woodville, discovered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a> in October 1464, and his refusal to follow Warwick's policy of closeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> led to Warwick's rebellion. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-anjou">Margaret of Anjou</a>, Warwick invaded in September 1470 and Henry VI was restored, while Edward fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. Edward returned with Burgundian aid, killed Warwick at Barnet, and on 4 May 1471 defeated Margaret's army at Tewkesbury, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s only son Edward of Westminster was killed.</p><p>Edward IV ruled for twelve more years. After his death in 1483 his twelve-year-old son Edward V reigned for 78 days before his uncle Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a>, amid controversy over the disappearance of Edward IV's two sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>, in exile in Brittany and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, returned and killed Richard at Bosworth Field in 1485.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Henry was crowned Henry VII on 30 October 1485 and married Elizabeth of York, Edward IV's eldest daughter, on 18 January 1486, uniting the two claims. Challengers remained, and the wars ended in 1487 with his defeat of the Yorkist opposition at Stoke Field. The Tudors ruled until 1603. Some historians, such as K. B. McFarlane, hold that the effects of the conflict on English society were exaggerated: many parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, East Anglia above all, were largely unaffected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wars of the Roses” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1461, during the <b>Wars of the Roses</b>, Mont Orgueil was handed to the French under an agreement made by Margaret of Anjou with Pierre de Brézé, and the island remained under French control until Sir Richard Harliston recovered it in 1468.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynastic <b>Wars of the Roses</b> were effectively decided by Henry Tudor’s victory at the Battle of Bosworth in 1485.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…March of the Men of Harlech&quot; (in Welsh &quot;Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr Harlech&quot;), is a song and military march traditionally said to tell of the siege of Harlech Castle from 1461 to 1468, when the castle was held for the Lancastrians against the Yorkists in the <b>Wars of the Roses</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Wars of the Roses</b>, a Yorkist army under John Neville, Baron Montagu, defeated a Lancastrian army under Henry Beaufort, Duke of Somerset, at Hedgeley Moor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His fall sowed the dynastic rivalry behind the <b>Wars of the Roses</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Wars of the Roses</b> were fought between the houses of York and Lancaster for the English throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
